#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064283 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064127 :question/id #uuid "373a63fc-3dae-5880-8a49-63b5c64888f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-734" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\sin^3{x}$, calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = (\\sin{x})^3.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = u^3, \\qquad u=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 3u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82c2e258-6d04-54d8-89db-2a8f5293bfff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos^{4} x\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063020} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a66b5-c7f8-56ce-bbe8-c842c252a9e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\cos^{4} x\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063021} {:answer/id #uuid "edf80007-6f09-5713-8251-369951eeb4c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\sin^{4} x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063022} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ef432a-1794-5152-82b8-729fc2eaec11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos x\\sin^{4} x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063023} {:answer/id #uuid "24572afc-e6f7-5ef5-8d37-f21b54435f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos^{4} x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063024}] :answer-field/id #uuid "aa8fcdb6-543b-5c70-a94e-8ee7a7f2c6ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063019}] :question/id #uuid "6bf091c6-c941-583b-9b04-914484ce090a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48562" :question/problem "Find $f^{′}(x)$ when $f(x) = \\cos^{5} (x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c16993-bae3-52c0-92ad-275cbeb50cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336262} {:answer/id #uuid "2e93c5d7-0745-5a8c-a9b3-de22c4366a79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336481} {:answer/id #uuid "61070e19-b4f6-5780-b52f-32136383a579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336909} {:answer/id #uuid "97d86c60-9434-5c84-85a6-a6f88a1b92d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337417} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a113ac-1f7d-54a3-aa47-90b10288ae2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e93c5d7-0745-5a8c-a9b3-de22c4366a79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336481} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a78cc6f-1a98-5e11-9c07-21ff7e2374f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338355}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "270b54b6-fdc6-5a75-af7c-f8052eee38ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-72154" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)=9{sin}^{2}⁡(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=9(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=9{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=18u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =18u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =18\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "903bc72c-9bed-5507-9ca2-4b5a360e273e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{csc}^{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "903bc72c-9bed-5507-9ca2-4b5a360e273e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{csc}^{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232206} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bb51450-3228-5dc2-96d0-2e4189e141cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232205}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f83dffe-8754-54db-b2a6-b0e1c17bfbe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-143516" :question/problem "The acceleration $a(t)$ of the particle is given by the derivative of the velocity with respect to time. Find the function $a(t).$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}t$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\nv(t)=4{csc}^{2}(t).\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $v(t)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nv=4{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{csc}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}=8u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{csc}t\\operatorname{cot}t.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =8u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}t\\operatorname{cot}t) \\\\  & =(8\\operatorname{csc}t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}t\\operatorname{cot}t) \\\\  & =-8{csc}^{2}t\\operatorname{cot}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f48db8cf-7b39-59af-a1cb-91ba855592e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6\\sin^2{(x)}\\cos{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f48db8cf-7b39-59af-a1cb-91ba855592e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6\\sin^2{(x)}\\cos{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232887} :answer-field/id #uuid "04dde564-b364-5b20-a216-7aeb3a48eaa7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232886}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45e23715-df02-5798-9f75-83ab7568fdb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-342413" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = -2\\sin^{3}{(x)},$ we have\n\n$f'(x) = $\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\\[\nf(x) = -2(\\sin{(x)})^{3}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\\[\nf = -2u^{3}, \\qquad u = \\sin{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm df}{\\textrm d u} = -6u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = -6u^2 \\cdot \\cos{(x)}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-6\\sin^2{(x)}\\cos{(x)}}.\n\\end{align*}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "489816c6-b47f-5da0-a223-5ac5074ad5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-7\\cos^{6}{(x)}\\sin{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "489816c6-b47f-5da0-a223-5ac5074ad5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-7\\cos^{6}{(x)}\\sin{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232889} :answer-field/id #uuid "31cc108a-77a1-5a3d-a2d2-0ce1e5364718" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232888}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d96ee12-4a46-5fb5-8b04-bb2200b126c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-342427" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\cos^{7}{(x)},$ we have\n\n$f'(x) = $\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\\[\nf(x) = (\\cos{(x)})^{7}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\\[\nf = u^{7}, \\qquad u = \\cos{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm d u} = 7u^6, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&= 7u^6 \\cdot (-\\sin{(x)})\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-7\\cos^6{(x)}\\sin{(x)}}.\n\\end{align*}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5afa7e6-c04f-5208-a625-922d31fe8db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{csc}^{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5afa7e6-c04f-5208-a625-922d31fe8db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{csc}^{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234934} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c8e574-e15b-5877-a041-10a0494c160b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235039}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40e69bfb-65e8-5b73-9c0a-94d7d3dd4ef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-167568" :question/problem "The acceleration $a(t)$ of the particle is given by the derivative of the velocity with respect to time. Find the function $a(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡t$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\nv(t)=2{csc}^{2}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $v(t)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nv=2{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}=4u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =4u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t) \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{csc}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4{csc}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18502f58-881d-5c04-adf0-1dff8233fb96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{sin}^{6}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18502f58-881d-5c04-adf0-1dff8233fb96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{sin}^{6}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315260} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed96f3cb-84c7-54a6-add8-c0c3c6980abb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317111}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e815036-fddb-52a7-beac-06443eed54ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-342420" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={sin}^{7}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{7},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=7{u}^{6},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =7{u}^{6}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =7{sin}^{6}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2991d8c4-2f44-5904-b476-71ee7a97e1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{cos}^{4}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2991d8c4-2f44-5904-b476-71ee7a97e1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{cos}^{4}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315355} :answer-field/id #uuid "47162fb1-7cc5-5c1b-9235-2c7a87d8e77b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316708}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "492e7737-f55c-5e21-8035-ce19a97914f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-342424" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=-4{cos}^{5}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))^{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-4{u}^{5},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-20{u}^{4},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-20{u}^{4}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =20{cos}^{4}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5072f597-c7ae-52a3-87d7-3ad34d6e5b09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cos}^{3}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327633} {:answer/id #uuid "6669dccf-ec8a-5aba-a5ba-e553646f661d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{sin}^{3}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327823} {:answer/id #uuid "775b7d53-b65b-56dc-b260-a851fc6a0b81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{sin}^{3}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327954} {:answer/id #uuid "833e7221-ef31-5abd-ab2b-ece81e8d01fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{cos}^{3}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328067} {:answer/id #uuid "bd72f829-edf9-5596-804b-df7af3cbc039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{cos}^{3}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5072f597-c7ae-52a3-87d7-3ad34d6e5b09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cos}^{3}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327633} :answer-field/id #uuid "554feb3a-de13-5dd4-8463-3992aa2f0689" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329338}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4df76406-4a28-5daf-b804-8ed5c07e252c" :question/math-academy-id "q-48385" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{4}⁡(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4{u}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-4{cos}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1030d421-1265-5bb8-baa7-de36cbf43dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327077} {:answer/id #uuid "21822650-4b61-54cf-b365-32887aa83c0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327224} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7cbdea-7d0a-584d-87c4-168bb3a5e9d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327303} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbf600c-1d4f-5579-b7ef-63e4631710e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328719} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b79361-896f-5fd9-b44f-d049b5991c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbbf600c-1d4f-5579-b7ef-63e4631710e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328719} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3634f3-1d3b-59bf-afa0-7e6b9b4131ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329516}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83266c61-bac6-595c-8c4f-6653bcc0f9f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48560" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=2u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "146ee98c-9e3d-59ea-b87c-00cdc34a5726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{sin}^{7}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336225} {:answer/id #uuid "14dd93d6-22de-5579-9c40-bd091e9348e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{7}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336229} {:answer/id #uuid "52065413-ca3d-5d6e-ba7f-0eae0320fd51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{7}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336786} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff0b52a-3ba0-5515-9ea8-71447c670910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40{cos}^{7}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337479} {:answer/id #uuid "de50a041-b819-5dde-87df-ba80e14ef96c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40{cos}^{7}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ff0b52a-3ba0-5515-9ea8-71447c670910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40{cos}^{7}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337479} :answer-field/id #uuid "9263207c-9def-540d-8075-a946dc50d58d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338548}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35acc32d-eaed-5047-8790-76ca5b79e5a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72156" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)=5{cos}^{8}⁡(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=5{u}^{8},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=40{u}^{7},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =40{u}^{7}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =40(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{7}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-40{cos}^{7}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b52aa7c5-0426-5772-8576-0d06507ee246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{6}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337642} {:answer/id #uuid "bca0ac11-5889-590d-b18f-dc821e5d6c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28{cos}^{6}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337708} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2962a1-647e-5d8b-9492-8e3b141a6e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28{sin}^{6}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338090} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f6bad8-7c0c-55c3-aec1-1f849a332783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{6}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338224} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8e2ee2-8bc8-5f48-a816-f19ea88144d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{cos}^{6}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bca0ac11-5889-590d-b18f-dc821e5d6c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28{cos}^{6}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337708} :answer-field/id #uuid "05250791-7660-5c5c-b045-5bac1563c939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338283}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e3f0d37-02ff-5992-af83-461909d51264" :question/math-academy-id "q-72157" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)=4{cos}^{7}⁡(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=4{u}^{7},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=28{u}^{6},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =28{u}^{6}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =28(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{6}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-28{cos}^{6}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48f64a5c-9e09-59b8-b17f-a73c876d2ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{sin}^{3}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48f64a5c-9e09-59b8-b17f-a73c876d2ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{sin}^{3}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490488} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbff81c-389a-5289-971d-8139b0252065" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490495}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "140dd45d-8107-5dae-9564-2bd6cb1a4f7b" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={sin}^{4}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{3}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ed3388-c369-5661-8011-9ad85b8a7185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{sin}^{5}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490485} {:answer/id #uuid "1fefddaa-9b00-592c-976c-b15111b07144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{cos}^{5}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490487} {:answer/id #uuid "4ccb1a92-1ab5-55f6-a051-1d41270340a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{cos}^{5}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490489} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a2b12a-811a-5b4b-be9f-b7b864c872c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{cos}^{5}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490494} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1ebebb-65a6-537a-9641-f6753ff84f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{sin}^{5}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7a2b12a-811a-5b4b-be9f-b7b864c872c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{cos}^{5}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490494} :answer-field/id #uuid "93ee8452-84d9-53fa-945b-db9c9f23d54d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490493}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1da4f94b-c6e6-57bb-95c9-efc33159af10" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{6}⁡(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{6},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=6{u}^{5}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =6{u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-6{u}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-6{cos}^{5}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67b6c87a-70ca-5491-baa9-b7ba9b113855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{cos}^{5}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67b6c87a-70ca-5491-baa9-b7ba9b113855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{cos}^{5}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490491} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e0e8dd8-ac9c-5253-9528-54ce170da5b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490492}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65409563-6aa3-541a-86d5-30b4fe746231" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=-2{cos}^{6}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))^{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-2{u}^{6},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-12{u}^{5},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-12{u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =12{cos}^{5}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064129 :question/id #uuid "4ebd0289-b278-5352-b7bd-10ae218c43fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-735" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y = \\tan{\\left(\\sin(x)\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan u, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec^2 u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048465 :topic/math-academy-id 268 :topic/title "Finding Trigonometric Ratios of Special Angles Using the Unit Circle"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8138f99-7ec9-5256-bd35-e33c7e848dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x\\cot^{2} (\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062468} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe44e20-3664-5950-8af4-4d1829f581e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x\\csc^{2} (\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062469} {:answer/id #uuid "ecca65a3-9b8e-59f8-b57a-ab7326ffb1c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\csc x\\sin^{2} (\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062470} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9ff75e-9b68-51c0-ab7d-23b5bb2fa18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x\\cos^{2} (\\csc x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062471} {:answer/id #uuid "679060c0-1bb6-505c-82ad-9ea21f50f18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\csc x\\cos^{2} (\\sin x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062472}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb1db77-5782-5f86-9fa6-af75143f4888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062467}] :question/id #uuid "2988d73e-07d4-547b-8f00-c916e255eb6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31356" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y = \\cot (\\cos x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b27ffe2-7676-50cf-8f85-63acc6816398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327316} {:answer/id #uuid "446323eb-ee99-55ba-b53a-c33b7f83bceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327531} {:answer/id #uuid "6e31cdb3-4a26-586a-a124-b350d2c5b39e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327889} {:answer/id #uuid "a5306166-8267-56b7-84f3-d958abd6020b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328359} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3ebbc8-ea7e-59c1-b950-2b9dc34de1a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "446323eb-ee99-55ba-b53a-c33b7f83bceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327531} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5003d8b-4b77-55dc-a36e-b7375c9d128f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329529}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55ba3ddf-3b81-51ce-a46b-d6facbbbea67" :question/math-academy-id "q-48643" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234097 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9728f53e-cf89-532c-9077-996c48020b2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48644" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y =\\cos \\left(\\sin x \\right)$ at the point $\\left(0,1 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 0$ is given by $f'(0).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\nf = \\cos{u}, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\sin{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =\\cos x.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\sin u \\cdot (\\cos x)\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\cos x \\sin (\\sin x).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\\[\n\t\\eqalign{\n\tf'(0)&=-\\cos (0) \\sin (\\sin (0))\\\\[3pt]\n\t&=-1 \\cdot 0 \\\\[3pt]\n        &=0.\n}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,1) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n\\begin{align}\ny-y_1 &= m(x-x_1)\\\\[3pt]\ny-1 &= (0)\\cdot\\left(x-0\\right)\\\\[3pt]\ny&=1.\n\\end{align}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234318 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3114b1b-5508-592e-9c4c-63d9fe2d48cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72163" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\operatorname{sin}(\\operatorname{sec}x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,u=\\operatorname{sec}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot (\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\operatorname{sec}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234319 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6630c4de-45f8-525a-9088-43f567b2047c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72170" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y =\\csc ( \\cos x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\csc{u}, \\qquad u = \\cos{x}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -\\csc{u}\\cot u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin{x}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=-\\csc u \\cot u \\cdot  (-\\sin x)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\sin x\\csc \\left(\\cos x\\right) \\cot \\left(\\cos x\\right).\n\\end{align*}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc286831-208d-5612-9363-4e8460161c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc286831-208d-5612-9363-4e8460161c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313569} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f72dc37-b228-560d-aba6-e98b5e17af4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "231f91ac-3de6-59a3-862f-b8057c01af56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "231f91ac-3de6-59a3-862f-b8057c01af56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312235} :answer-field/id #uuid "a272334b-048a-545c-b7bd-74f9c4889490" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314448}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76019817-2e4b-589f-ae43-b8959040e2aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-341223" :question/problem "Given that $y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b3638de-2232-5b1b-8cad-82466c730a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}(\\operatorname{tan}(x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b3638de-2232-5b1b-8cad-82466c730a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}(\\operatorname{tan}(x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315180} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8797a85-728c-5c21-bc99-32e06307e7d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317133}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6aceca48-ebb2-5f8b-b98b-8c687aa13f66" :question/math-academy-id "q-342436" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={sec}^{2}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b31fbe-14ab-5c3d-8b9a-02859f8e2640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23b31fbe-14ab-5c3d-8b9a-02859f8e2640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315317} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a4e064c-845a-59e5-8333-2b01b7ac8e6d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "937de51f-ef0a-5577-95ef-6a8fef931e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "937de51f-ef0a-5577-95ef-6a8fef931e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315940} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad5dcd71-ae03-574c-b8dd-2c78211d0666" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316953} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7553de-ae66-58dc-a54c-8fcb1941ce52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a7553de-ae66-58dc-a54c-8fcb1941ce52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315454} :answer-field/id #uuid "f850e3ea-810b-5928-bf54-2533be4452a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317131}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50d04d8e-3b54-51a1-977a-8035c4a5ed2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342443" :question/problem "Given that $y=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sec}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)\\operatorname{tan}⁡($\n{{field-3}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd80f4fc-d66d-5918-905b-16bb0d321fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd80f4fc-d66d-5918-905b-16bb0d321fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316535} :answer-field/id #uuid "28742ecc-1707-5ebf-af66-6910320113a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316640} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb68a3a2-59aa-56c5-bc1b-c4aa4acceb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb68a3a2-59aa-56c5-bc1b-c4aa4acceb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316268} :answer-field/id #uuid "95746905-c009-54be-9389-495c9c01a41a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316888}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36a01249-dd25-5201-9ce2-0ee325c40559" :question/math-academy-id "q-342445" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x))\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x))\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad365d4-359a-52c4-805a-c1687b51bf98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336430} {:answer/id #uuid "646e64f6-f63d-5208-9dad-bae8b2423826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336932} {:answer/id #uuid "785f85d3-99ed-5869-bb7a-14c4604ec1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337115} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2e4cd6-fb63-51c7-b9f4-288b7402ef9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337160} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ac1cf5-ef67-52e6-b531-518c01f08c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "646e64f6-f63d-5208-9dad-bae8b2423826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336932} :answer-field/id #uuid "18999e45-9b70-57cd-8509-bc4dc9842011" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338319}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "363aed62-f3dd-56da-b30d-2474bf68073a" :question/math-academy-id "q-72161" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35d0b5a0-960c-537b-b36b-f81b09719172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336532} {:answer/id #uuid "8659cf38-7f28-521b-9eb6-a1f4bb5d55a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337248} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2b4496-4d43-50c5-b8f4-50cbe3748941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337845} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a21dd0-44ef-54d6-bc1c-1c98a20cbcd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338037} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b439b8-4fb4-5bd2-b82d-c5283f54f75b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b439b8-4fb4-5bd2-b82d-c5283f54f75b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338204} :answer-field/id #uuid "76164faf-c99c-514d-8f55-17d4f55b16c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338480}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdadc1fd-0dca-59ce-bd49-88a477cdb0a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-72173" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡u,\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd535f0f-13a3-59e8-aa98-e4bebe36408f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd535f0f-13a3-59e8-aa98-e4bebe36408f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484791} :answer-field/id #uuid "939e86f3-e069-59f1-87f9-c70b1bca8329" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484795}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "30a0323d-00da-5727-8bed-8f4570018874" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cos{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4} \\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWrite the right-hand side of the equation in terms of $x$.\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484800 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a1c429d-91c5-5bb7-af02-633485681a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a1c429d-91c5-5bb7-af02-633485681a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484788} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf12cc56-7e9d-5f32-968f-6c8272d67936" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484796}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53218be1-6a3c-5560-9338-ef81bd9ab181" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cos{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWrite the right-hand side of the equation in terms of $x$.\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=- \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bac1832-cbc0-5239-b224-d88d18d436fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bac1832-cbc0-5239-b224-d88d18d436fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484789} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c500a86-e1db-5337-a98a-9655641e4016" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484793}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9c2a70b8-20d0-5b7c-a81b-d0ca3277e712" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cos{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWrite the right-hand side of the equation in terms of $x$.\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{3}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{3})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{3})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}.$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76829e80-fc0b-5c20-83fa-559e054cce93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76829e80-fc0b-5c20-83fa-559e054cce93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484790} :answer-field/id #uuid "f03b9128-e960-5087-b4b2-f78af1a3ef3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484797}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfc21866-9683-5244-ad3b-eca7c1a8e833" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cos{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWrite the right-hand side of the equation in terms of $x$.\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{4})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{4})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cffb8108-0a8b-529c-831d-d01364b7996c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cffb8108-0a8b-529c-831d-d01364b7996c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484792} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aec45a1-2e42-5a0f-985b-263a94020dc4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484794}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f5d87654-628a-541b-9ab2-cf93106a65ac" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cos{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3} \\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWrite the right-hand side of the equation in terms of $x$.\n\n$y=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $f'(0)=-\\sin(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}.$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096541 :question/id #uuid "b00f1213-cdaf-512c-9842-92cbee1ec63a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6100" :question/problem "Differentiate $y = \\cos^4{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny = u^4, \\qquad u = \\cos{v}, \\qquad v = 2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 4u^3,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d v} = -\\sin{v},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm d x} = 2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =-8{cos}^{3}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049332 :topic/math-academy-id 1036 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Logarithmic Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271 :topic/math-academy-id 1007 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789 :topic/math-academy-id 305 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Trigonometric Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084 :topic/math-academy-id 1108 :topic/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc9442cc-8d33-595e-9e41-73b4047b032d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\cos^{2} 3x\\sin 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095413} {:answer/id #uuid "fcdbd89b-535d-5d97-8858-715b0e7cbab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\cos^{2} x\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095414} {:answer/id #uuid "fd99476d-2a11-5897-b1d5-46451cb0f160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sin^{2} 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095415} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1e2fac-0635-5edf-ab7e-8243187ee585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sin^{2} 3x\\cos 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095416} {:answer/id #uuid "6a521037-9532-52ff-8aae-92cf38bd8c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos^{2} 3x\\sin 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095417}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1e73542d-ef52-5a7f-9676-f9cd21e1ce6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095412}] :question/id #uuid "287eb86d-de85-5a68-b478-fc3615b0b846" :question/math-academy-id "q-48569" :question/problem "Find $f^{′}(x)$ when $f(x) = \\cos^{3} 3x$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5d6aa5c-e7ec-5f27-9f30-0940fb6f5772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sin^{2} 4x\\cos 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095419} {:answer/id #uuid "eb26a196-10da-5266-a0a6-0432f318280e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin^{2} 4x\\cos 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095420} {:answer/id #uuid "140c8dbe-118e-5697-ab9d-2530699d840b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sin^{2} x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095421} {:answer/id #uuid "52293fad-13c6-5edb-bda4-a70cc3f23bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sin 4x\\cos 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095422} {:answer/id #uuid "6af1efa8-0299-5477-97d4-c6cf5ea116b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095423}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fa0a0398-2eac-50f7-a78d-91ba28b54adb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095418}] :question/id #uuid "bf61b2d5-63df-5002-abfc-0d0c2d68ffa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-48570" :question/problem "Find $f^{′}(x)$ when $f(x) = \\sin^{3} 4x$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233557 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d32bf1a7-eae8-544d-b18f-ee99dc9e7258" :question/math-academy-id "q-16699" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(1)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{3}(5{x}^{2}+2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{3},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}v,\\,v=5{x}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=3{u}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-\\operatorname{sin}v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=10x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}v)\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}(5{x}^{2}+2))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{2}+2))\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =-30x{cos}^{2}(5{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{2}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{'}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(1) & =-30(1){cos}^{2}(5(1)^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}(5(1)^{2}+2) \\\\  & =-30{cos}^{2}7\\operatorname{sin}7\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "584f034a-e243-5e92-85ec-d412187c42f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "584f034a-e243-5e92-85ec-d412187c42f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232542} :answer-field/id #uuid "abcda024-6128-542d-bc71-d5c1255b256a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232541}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d78ed24f-7cdd-50da-a7c5-8326d3933908" :question/math-academy-id "q-312907" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{8}(5{x}^{3}).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} ${cos}^{7}(5{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{3})$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{8},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}v,\\,v=5{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=8{u}^{7},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-\\operatorname{sin}v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=15{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =8{u}^{7}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}v)\\cdot 15{x}^{2} \\\\  & =8{(\\operatorname{cos}(5{x}^{3}))}^{7}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{3}))\\cdot 15{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-120{x}^{2}{cos}^{7}(5{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f0c7111-a060-5641-9f28-08ebb3f97820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54x{sin}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288462} {:answer/id #uuid "4bff2b13-d34a-5a2a-ab84-1110b5438aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{sin}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288558} {:answer/id #uuid "9a36fc3b-3a7c-5fac-bbfb-9178461a03fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18x{sin}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289063} {:answer/id #uuid "b0394bcf-fb4f-5bf6-9de5-f7e8cc6ee14a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18x{sin}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289196} {:answer/id #uuid "d6add1df-78d9-5682-b6d5-4c7d9b74e6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54x{sin}^{3}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\,{cos}^{3}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f0c7111-a060-5641-9f28-08ebb3f97820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54x{sin}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288462} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a37d7f9-8684-5d8d-b945-6f277a28d504" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289997}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9cfcb56-203d-51a2-aa05-78075c70500a" :question/math-academy-id "q-2397" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={sin}^{3}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{3},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,v=9{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=3{u}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=18x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)\\cdot 18x \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1))^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1))\\cdot 18x \\\\  & =54x{sin}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0480da11-736f-54eb-8f1c-b45c5eafb77b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295288} {:answer/id #uuid "656e07a7-3c98-59bf-85d3-2d52b695e711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295845} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf163c6-73b7-53e4-8558-870d5120e61c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296098} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e00c3b-4340-5c91-be7a-047cb1624ae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296136} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fe8de4-4953-53e2-babf-0316522289bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0480da11-736f-54eb-8f1c-b45c5eafb77b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295288} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b048a01-7c6a-5074-8040-31f2a7ab3a71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296859}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0b4e552-dbf9-536f-981b-a75efbb821de" :question/math-academy-id "q-28655" :question/problem "Find ${h}^{′}(0)$ when $h(x)={5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $h$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nh={5}^{u},\\,u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\,v=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\frac{1}{v},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{v})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${h}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(0) & =-{5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\  & =-{5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a923d0d-683f-5a22-8b67-4a4f6919866f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(x+6){csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295424} {:answer/id #uuid "272999f6-d0d3-5ba0-9d45-afe5af8fefcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295481} {:answer/id #uuid "a23f01d4-b0cf-5d81-b3a4-2e37d3003baf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296131} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4d398b-f413-539d-9058-6015c9708ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(x+6)\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296244} {:answer/id #uuid "e1aa6a79-375b-56fc-a7eb-84281d9d15fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(x+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a923d0d-683f-5a22-8b67-4a4f6919866f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(x+6){csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295424} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cd72a85-2293-547b-8a73-3db56c2ab7b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296688}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c9a1630-1e07-5782-a0c4-5e97e3d68c4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-31352" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ when $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡[(x+6)^{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u={v}^{2},\\,v=x+6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=2v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2(x+6){csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143d951a-1e7a-598c-ab01-6ac4eebb972d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295381} {:answer/id #uuid "80ac022d-2fa2-5312-b6bb-20edccc427c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{cot}^{3}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295959} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd53f52-57a0-58c2-84e5-51e7836dcaa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{2}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296358} {:answer/id #uuid "df819a99-e827-5e8d-8038-4bdc1f81e2b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296451} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c37201-fff5-5a86-9be9-3c9d741d2eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "143d951a-1e7a-598c-ab01-6ac4eebb972d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295381} :answer-field/id #uuid "c18783dd-cacf-52c8-94df-17f7aaad8258" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297033}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "60550674-e8b4-5f20-9b57-5b71c82e6894" :question/math-academy-id "q-31361" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={cot}^{4}⁡(5x-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=5x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{csc}^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡(5x-1))\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(5x-1){csc}^{2}⁡(5x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(5(0)-1){csc}^{2}⁡(5(0)-1) \\\\  & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(-1){csc}^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-20(-{cot}^{3}⁡1){csc}^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3d2230-dda0-54b6-89ba-24372639fa9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d3d2230-dda0-54b6-89ba-24372639fa9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315198} :answer-field/id #uuid "72951b74-10fa-525b-ae61-0c5a5cf05a42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316821}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c163105-12ab-5d59-bb79-bbc5b4799b09" :question/math-academy-id "q-342927" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ when $y=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{u},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,v=2x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1)}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1)}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e7b9d4-67a3-5775-a5d2-ca4446d5b44e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sin^{2} 4x\\cos 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489397} {:answer/id #uuid "59290209-9482-50f4-9098-13bb16954c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\cos^{2} x\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489406} {:answer/id #uuid "80239181-a206-540d-80dd-4c77a3b494f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sin^{2} 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489417} {:answer/id #uuid "dac562ba-737c-5fb3-9e6d-34d15181372b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\cos^{2} 4x\\sin 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489437} {:answer/id #uuid "f14bda28-1914-5bb5-b3c9-d58a85885cd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos^{2} 4x\\sin 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dac562ba-737c-5fb3-9e6d-34d15181372b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\cos^{2} 4x\\sin 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489437} :answer-field/id #uuid "031b15e0-3048-56cd-bce5-35907b4c2ec6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489389}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b547b24-9888-53eb-ab95-e124d3f31432" :question/problem "Find $f^{′}(x)$ when $f(x) = \\cos^{3} 4x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=u^3,\\,u=\\cos v,\\,v=4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=3u^2,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}=-\\sin v,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\\\\n&=3u^2\\cdot (-\\sin v)\\cdot 4\\\\\n&=-12\\cos^{2} 4x\\sin 4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac2a3d6-1261-55ae-9429-ab0a45e319bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{sin}^{3}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,{cos}^{3}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489395} {:answer/id #uuid "3979ed87-af85-57db-8316-21223ec16943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{sin}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489399} {:answer/id #uuid "5326b014-bdff-590c-91fd-da791559ff94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{sin}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489405} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c86454-1fbf-57d8-9fab-f0e9a82e9af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{sin}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,{cos}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489443} {:answer/id #uuid "f7dc7484-013b-5097-9937-5df5cca67819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{sin}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7dc7484-013b-5097-9937-5df5cca67819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{sin}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489449} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31723c2-df16-5a29-a9cf-4ab545f80f99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489424}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2168b13d-cc27-5e92-a481-beee5381eccc" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={sin}^{3}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{3},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,v=1{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=3{u}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1))^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1))\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6x{sin}^{2}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1{x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "645f8651-c907-5897-a0f8-5495b4696424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sin 3x\\cos 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489409} {:answer/id #uuid "88fccb2c-a82c-5005-b096-ec2ab5d38a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sin^{2} x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489420} {:answer/id #uuid "e4660400-a1f8-5409-a2f2-c6cebf3a25e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin^{2} 3x\\cos 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489444} {:answer/id #uuid "e9377be7-0c61-5ada-a744-bc51453850a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489446} {:answer/id #uuid "feca9f99-8e26-59c1-8151-7e33c2d68ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sin^{2} 3x\\cos 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feca9f99-8e26-59c1-8151-7e33c2d68ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sin^{2} 3x\\cos 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489451} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eb18e9f-7b04-574c-bf8c-fb35e930eba6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489416}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69eb6901-57ef-5c23-9e79-478c120da8a0" :question/problem "Find $f^{′}(x)$ when $f(x) = \\sin^{3} 3x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=u^3,\\,u=\\sin v,\\,v=3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=3u^2,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}=\\cos v,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\\\\n&=3u^2\\cdot (\\cos v)\\cdot 3\\\\\n&=9\\sin^{2} 3x\\cos 3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1578d2e7-e3c7-58ca-ae2c-e000eee7b584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\cos^{2} x\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489391} {:answer/id #uuid "31dbc390-d7ff-52b7-9554-7039ff3e33a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin^{2} 2x\\cos 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489398} {:answer/id #uuid "a6daa534-1144-57a1-bda8-9347e8f3c975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos^{2} 2x\\sin 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489425} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9b90a3-6e76-58c1-b372-848a72b05143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sin^{2} 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489428} {:answer/id #uuid "e071ac82-c30e-510e-891d-7e6d4aab4fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\cos^{2} 2x\\sin 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e071ac82-c30e-510e-891d-7e6d4aab4fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\cos^{2} 2x\\sin 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489438} :answer-field/id #uuid "e16ae766-5a2f-5472-aaef-e0f1cfc54863" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489440}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78f271bc-8d43-5a38-8524-01c52619086f" :question/problem "Find $f^{′}(x)$ when $f(x) = \\cos^{3} 2x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=u^3,\\,u=\\cos v,\\,v=2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=3u^2,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}=-\\sin v,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\\\\n&=3u^2\\cdot (-\\sin v)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=-6\\cos^{2} 2x\\sin 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e198e28-7ad4-54be-9fe1-0ede79594f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin^{2} 2x\\cos 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489401} {:answer/id #uuid "6b326e02-a18a-5405-9760-a7eadc21044f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489411} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa31131-7bc2-5861-b733-db93afc59ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin^{2} 2x\\cos 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489421} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fffbf4-1840-5e14-8dd6-a5d71b97666c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin 2x\\cos 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489439} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e13535-c174-5ff3-a658-d532f5876d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin^{2} x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e198e28-7ad4-54be-9fe1-0ede79594f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin^{2} 2x\\cos 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489401} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb2277f3-ece1-5aa4-a6b5-143a44b7c878" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489435}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b67a6cf9-dc23-5ea7-a514-a5dcfabdbd86" :question/problem "Find $f^{′}(x)$ when $f(x) = \\sin^{3} 2x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=u^3,\\,u=\\sin v,\\,v=2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=3u^2,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}=\\cos v,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\\\\n&=3u^2\\cdot (\\cos v)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=6\\sin^{2} 2x\\cos 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c23db35-1c82-59b5-adbb-2c1801e613c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489407} {:answer/id #uuid "607877ee-30d1-5662-a148-072bd2a65e8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489408} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbdb636-8a72-52ec-ab3b-ff891686c910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489413} {:answer/id #uuid "868c8189-a478-5120-ad8e-fdc97fe80ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{2}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489419} {:answer/id #uuid "c4041f6b-566b-50c0-84f6-8e034c917e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{cot}^{3}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c23db35-1c82-59b5-adbb-2c1801e613c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489407} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ee0f43d-3c4a-51a8-975c-eb46b3a70f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489402}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3baed33-1cfd-5982-8e88-c33747aa34cf" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={cot}^{4}⁡(2x-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=2x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{csc}^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡(2x-1))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8{cot}^{3}⁡(2x-1){csc}^{2}⁡(2x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-8{cot}^{3}⁡(2(0)-1){csc}^{2}⁡(2(0)-1) \\\\  & =-8{cot}^{3}⁡(-1){csc}^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-8(-{cot}^{3}⁡1){csc}^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =8{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2970eeb1-3b49-50db-9453-4ae0d788a789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489394} {:answer/id #uuid "83cf7332-9e22-5d6b-ac71-dbdbc51417e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{cot}^{3}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489418} {:answer/id #uuid "c620ccb3-5b19-5217-abfc-3a07d7d021d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489434} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a96852-18db-5426-80b3-8889b50de605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{2}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489436} {:answer/id #uuid "e235745d-204e-54fb-993d-8da177d2d7b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c620ccb3-5b19-5217-abfc-3a07d7d021d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489434} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe24ada9-bd92-5b0d-bbee-0258963f8532" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489450}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c51262c0-6413-5083-b852-6971681819a1" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={cot}^{4}⁡(3x-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=3x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{csc}^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡(3x-1))\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12{cot}^{3}⁡(3x-1){csc}^{2}⁡(3x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-12{cot}^{3}⁡(3(0)-1){csc}^{2}⁡(3(0)-1) \\\\  & =-12{cot}^{3}⁡(-1){csc}^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-12(-{cot}^{3}⁡1){csc}^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =12{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "420bec15-5b1c-5a49-848e-cd7d88c0122d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos^{2} 5x\\sin 5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489403} {:answer/id #uuid "51162c0e-e911-55b6-b2f6-ee71df4f9905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15\\cos^{2} x\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489404} {:answer/id #uuid "9c33688a-a6ce-5589-b010-ce864fe74a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15\\cos^{2} 5x\\sin 5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489423} {:answer/id #uuid "c14927a1-839b-53c3-a003-77536cb03a57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sin^{2} 5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489429} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e322d2-fdf0-5a97-8851-f93a72dcad20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\sin^{2} 5x\\cos 5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c33688a-a6ce-5589-b010-ce864fe74a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15\\cos^{2} 5x\\sin 5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489423} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c1f8536-cd5e-57c1-98bd-f1d312bce9b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489412}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7f20887-76fb-5d13-bcfc-20da4d07fad6" :question/problem "Find $f^{′}(x)$ when $f(x) = \\cos^{3} 5x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=u^3,\\,u=\\cos v,\\,v=5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=3u^2,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}=-\\sin v,\\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}v}\\cdot\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\\\\n&=3u^2\\cdot (-\\sin v)\\cdot 5\\\\\n&=-15\\cos^{2} 5x\\sin 5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22005ee4-41d0-5760-b893-f0fac19db39a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489393} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2a9975-b462-5030-bd6f-57419893c0e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1{cot}^{3}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489396} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd77f99-da27-58dd-a347-1c31d50cedc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489414} {:answer/id #uuid "9249d26c-d661-549a-91a4-caf233a82221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{2}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489422} {:answer/id #uuid "af8ef9cf-cd77-54c5-ad2b-ba9478fe6d6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fd77f99-da27-58dd-a347-1c31d50cedc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489414} :answer-field/id #uuid "3abb3881-a4da-56ea-afd6-a57f4bafd656" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489400}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f751f15c-49b5-572a-960c-e6e9fe0fd73f" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={cot}^{4}⁡(1x-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=1x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{csc}^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(1x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡(1x-1))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4{cot}^{3}⁡(1x-1){csc}^{2}⁡(1x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-4{cot}^{3}⁡(1(0)-1){csc}^{2}⁡(1(0)-1) \\\\  & =-4{cot}^{3}⁡(-1){csc}^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-4(-{cot}^{3}⁡1){csc}^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096404 :question/id #uuid "a2bb6a33-8a57-5b6b-9d35-cf611fbd5c9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-185" :question/problem "If $f(x) = e^{-2x}\\tan({x^2})$, then what is $f'(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $v(x)=\\tan{(x^2)}.$ Then\n\n$$\nu'(x) = -2e^{-2x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2x\\sec^2(x^2).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate both $u$ and $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(-2{e}^{-2x})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+{e}^{-2x}\\cdot 2x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})+x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}(x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813 :topic/math-academy-id 1109 :topic/title "The Product Rule for Differentiation"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1e3fd3-5561-53bf-af94-2a5a56a62b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12x^{4} + x^{2})(2x^{2} + 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093867} {:answer/id #uuid "529892d3-fe64-52a5-9433-8978cd66fb3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12x^{4} + 3x^{2})(2x^{2} + 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093868} {:answer/id #uuid "5a109ebf-3786-5269-8945-bc9bc7404324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(18x^{4} + 3x^{2})(2x^{2} + 1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093869} {:answer/id #uuid "68c92ca2-1813-5e7f-851a-bc0bbc3787a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(18x^{4} + 3x^{2})(2x^{2} + 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093870} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4336dc-86ac-5e7f-88fc-c903df1cb1eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x^{4} + 12x^{2})(2x^{2} + 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093871}] :answer-field/id #uuid "66469be4-8083-5074-a455-8084fdaf504e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093866}] :question/id #uuid "e3114326-74b1-5fc2-91eb-5c3ecb44b5e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-11599" :question/problem "If $y = x^{3}(2x^{2} + 1)^{3}$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a44fe7a2-5e5a-5eb8-805d-7b829fc0d942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos 1 + 2\\sin 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095244} {:answer/id #uuid "b40b5c59-e2e7-53be-8069-7947875a60a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos 1 + 2\\sin 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095245} {:answer/id #uuid "db1afcf3-0d9f-569f-a120-5478079a6104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos 1 + \\sin 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095246} {:answer/id #uuid "f98a997b-dd95-5cc3-9f1d-cafac532e26a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos 1 + 4\\sin 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095247} {:answer/id #uuid "48f9401d-07df-5bd6-beef-7e93a2c1e92b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos 5 + 2\\sin 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095248}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9708991f-d3ee-5d1d-b2fe-2d10b43d24eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095243}] :question/id #uuid "b0691861-26bf-593b-966e-465050f7203f" :question/math-academy-id "q-31240" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y = 2x\\cos (2x + 3)$ at $x =-1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233226 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75157a6e-bf73-5daf-9fa9-84f0f942f081" :question/math-academy-id "q-11602" :question/problem "If $y=2{x}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7},$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=2{x}^{3}$ and $v(x)=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=6{x}^{2},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(6{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7})+(2{x}^{3})(\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}}) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}+\\frac{4{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=2} & =6(2)^{2}\\sqrt{2(2)^{2}+7}+\\frac{4(2)^{4}}{\\sqrt{2(2)^{2}+7}} \\\\  & =24\\sqrt{15}+\\frac{64}{\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{360+64}{\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{424}{\\sqrt{15}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233227 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e995c495-b172-523a-b382-0b2801a175c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-11603" :question/problem "If $y=3{x}^{2}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}$, find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=6x,\\,{v}^{'}(x)=12{x}^{2}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(6x)(({x}^{3}-1)^{4})+(3{x}^{2})(12{x}^{2}({x}^{3}-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =6x({x}^{3}-1)^{4}+36{x}^{4}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(6x({x}^{3}-1)+36{x}^{4})\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(6{x}^{4}-6x+36{x}^{4})\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(42{x}^{4}-6x)\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=1$, we substitute $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=1} & =(42\\cdot {1}^{4}-6\\cdot 1){({1}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =36\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233267 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "429c60b9-5038-573f-9e54-efbf678a8db5" :question/math-academy-id "q-11939" :question/problem "Given that $y=x\\operatorname{ln}3x,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}3x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{ln}3x)+(x)(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}3x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec192c70-e569-50ff-8384-af86c7a4c091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t\\operatorname{sin}(3t)+3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec192c70-e569-50ff-8384-af86c7a4c091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t\\operatorname{sin}(3t)+3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232208} :answer-field/id #uuid "591ef578-25d3-5e0e-9800-62864943c1c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232207}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86ba5562-5090-57e8-ba8d-4732a237a9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143518" :question/problem "Find the acceleration function ${a}_{2}(t)$ of the second particle.\n\n${a}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 3, we saw that the derivative of a particle's velocity with respect to time gives its acceleration.\n\nTo differentiate ${v}_{2}(t),$ we require the product rule. Let $g(t)={t}^{2}$ and $h(t)=\\operatorname{sin}(3t).$ Then\n\n$$\n{g}^{'}(t)=2t,\\,{h}^{'}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}(3t).\n$$\n\nWe used the chain rule to calculate ${h}^{'}(t).$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2}(t) & ={v}_{2}^{'}(t) \\\\  & ={g}^{'}(t)\\cdot h(t)+g(t)\\cdot {h}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(2t)(\\operatorname{sin}(3t))+({t}^{2})(3\\operatorname{cos}(3t)) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{sin}(3t)+3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233556 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e685a2e1-a3e0-5b0b-8629-577bd827ece1" :question/math-academy-id "q-16698" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}3x.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}3x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}3x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{cos}3x)+({e}^{x})(-3\\operatorname{sin}3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}3x-3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}3x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}3x-3\\operatorname{sin}3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "4aac1b00-ccaf-57eb-98c1-277328cf34e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232543}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd31dbf5-1a9b-54a3-a636-334695ca85d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-313299" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=2x,\\,{v}^{'}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}2x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(2x)(\\operatorname{sin}2x)+({x}^{2})(2\\operatorname{cos}2x) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}2x+2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a14ea0-cb64-5f19-aac2-523068cc3521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}x+\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05a14ea0-cb64-5f19-aac2-523068cc3521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}x+\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232899} :answer-field/id #uuid "60ccd4ce-b471-58b7-b799-fc85b9ac5283" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232898}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3bdb90b1-e345-5eb6-a4f8-ee5a314e57ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-343333" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={e}^{5x}\\operatorname{ln}x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=($\n{{field-1}} $){e}^{5x}$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={e}^{5x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=5{e}^{5x},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(5{e}^{5x})(\\operatorname{ln}x)+({e}^{5x})\\,(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =(5\\operatorname{ln}x+\\frac{1}{x}){e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d0654e0-d49d-534f-9b33-68e3e971d9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(t/2)-t/2\\operatorname{sin}(t/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d0654e0-d49d-534f-9b33-68e3e971d9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(t/2)-t/2\\operatorname{sin}(t/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234758} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdafbc71-e376-54dc-999f-eac8f84227b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235208}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3dd0c3db-6967-5a89-a656-a9043eef1f70" :question/math-academy-id "q-167572" :question/problem "Find the acceleration function ${a}_{2}(t)$ of the second particle.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${a}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 3, we saw that the derivative of a particle's velocity with respect to time gives its acceleration.\n\nTo differentiate ${v}_{2}(t),$ we require the product rule. Let $g(t)=t$ and $h(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Then\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=1,\\,{h}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe used the chain rule to calculate ${h}^{′}(t).$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2}(t) & ={v}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)+g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))+(t)(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237779 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c304fc9-c9da-5d97-9759-ccf6e138d12f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236194} {:answer/id #uuid "53809d10-dcef-5e85-886e-cc325cd763be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236347} {:answer/id #uuid "77c7b91a-bbc1-589a-884f-c42b2bb99fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236569} {:answer/id #uuid "78657ffb-16ea-5821-a776-4f1b2747a9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236573} {:answer/id #uuid "c385379d-8237-5252-97dc-f26a22711a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53809d10-dcef-5e85-886e-cc325cd763be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236347} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a6e6951-f040-51c5-a0b5-e70540d8f884" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237642}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc8a355a-ee17-5da1-9a5f-fc0c8e06bcf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-10009" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u$ and $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=\\pi /2} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46092d6a-5809-530b-96aa-d18ca741a149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9x+1){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251132} {:answer/id #uuid "9449d2c4-40c8-5886-a8f8-d377e92abd71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9x+10){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251828} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcc1d83-99af-5f5a-b373-703da0e18e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251929} {:answer/id #uuid "a8022752-cd20-552c-942e-08b64f288227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9x{e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252002} {:answer/id #uuid "af7653ed-b253-50e2-9a68-8c72c94c9870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(x+1){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9449d2c4-40c8-5886-a8f8-d377e92abd71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9x+10){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251828} :answer-field/id #uuid "2531d2a5-3c0e-51d6-8cfe-475ebfb26445" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252873}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab66beff-1db7-5265-a536-ea4d30aeb123" :question/math-academy-id "q-11824" :question/problem "Given that $y=(x+1){e}^{9x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=x+1$ and $v(x)={e}^{9x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=9{e}^{9x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(1)({e}^{9x})+(x+1)(9{e}^{9x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{9x}+9(x+1){e}^{9x} \\\\  & =(1+9(x+1)){e}^{9x} \\\\  & =(9x+10){e}^{9x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e395c8-f565-5740-959d-6bf0c34f8710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡a\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288420} {:answer/id #uuid "47d8a58a-8445-5585-bc47-18b1d4683989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288526} {:answer/id #uuid "60da0eec-e09f-556c-977c-0afa9bca8118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288710} {:answer/id #uuid "9efacfe8-4ef9-586f-935e-d85e18391332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡a\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289098} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f42cff-705d-5052-8de2-d67ef0b14a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sec}^{2}⁡x+3{a}^{3x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38e395c8-f565-5740-959d-6bf0c34f8710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡a\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288420} :answer-field/id #uuid "84733a66-1c91-5acb-9908-573c1e35d199" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290021}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4cb91b7-6460-5845-ba44-f35610f01889" :question/math-academy-id "q-2392" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={a}^{3x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $v(x)={a}^{3x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=3{a}^{3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡a.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡x)({a}^{3x})+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(3{a}^{3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡a) \\\\  & ={a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡a\\operatorname{tan}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25db7b46-2d5d-595a-8da7-747d2ddd12b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288282} {:answer/id #uuid "5d85a103-3ef8-59d4-8e84-637387fa0c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}-x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288686} {:answer/id #uuid "922058af-c9bd-5099-865a-eed92655f212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289014} {:answer/id #uuid "99fc2ea7-0124-5d04-bc08-8eef810aff45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289061} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a9b433-04de-5c42-b250-5744d10efff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}+8x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99fc2ea7-0124-5d04-bc08-8eef810aff45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289061} :answer-field/id #uuid "04b0a0d1-3edc-5da1-b114-a49367e0bb38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289737}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5501845-7745-5f41-81f4-6fab62788d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-2401" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=4{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=8x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(8x)(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})+(4{x}^{2})(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}) \\\\  & =8x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44207d6e-a407-5515-8b9e-f67da1b5db73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}4x+4\\operatorname{cos}4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44207d6e-a407-5515-8b9e-f67da1b5db73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}4x+4\\operatorname{cos}4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315515} :answer-field/id #uuid "165c0e8f-7c32-58b2-bd8b-4210c1bded3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316597}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8a4f8c2-ffbe-520c-8f0b-03285d5b4493" :question/math-academy-id "q-343332" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=($\n{{field-1}} $){e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cc31045-6721-506d-b85c-975fcd9e3e95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cc31045-6721-506d-b85c-975fcd9e3e95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315556} :answer-field/id #uuid "7be93c96-933c-5510-b979-c109a0def8ed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316843}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "658890fd-175e-5961-9b65-48efc632b168" :question/math-academy-id "q-343354" :question/problem "Given that $y=2x{e}^{6x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=($\n{{field-1}} $){e}^{6x}$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=2x$ and $v(x)={e}^{6x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=6{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2)({e}^{6x})+(2x)(6{e}^{6x}) \\\\  & =(12x+2){e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20f4b495-e5fb-55a4-b9fd-82586fb5a57e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360977} {:answer/id #uuid "553cde80-173f-5c83-990b-953883f6a78f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361220} {:answer/id #uuid "6048e77d-cff3-5a2c-a13d-9857f23b54a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361271} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3805d8-997f-5a7d-a009-fb4112187474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361319} {:answer/id #uuid "c0543623-6d50-5d27-965b-5debe119e83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c3805d8-997f-5a7d-a009-fb4112187474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361319} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d57a36-0a6c-5441-82df-3d718e622317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362018}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a85bd741-601d-56b9-acc2-25f554507b6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-9942" :question/problem "If $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2).$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2))+({x}^{2})(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2)) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+2)+5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2089f74f-7bb1-531f-9f54-4e2969f07e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360974} {:answer/id #uuid "54599985-5dd3-502a-b9ef-88b1d618a930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361211} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9b1f0e-03d5-557f-83d3-fc825eebaa97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361263} {:answer/id #uuid "72ddff39-ee62-599f-a890-d26c22490faf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361340} {:answer/id #uuid "93d239b8-90ad-5454-b0f7-400662e44c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9b1f0e-03d5-557f-83d3-fc825eebaa97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361263} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03caba5-0350-5e9d-aa7c-ebd7a638e046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362179}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25941eb3-76ad-51ee-8456-c5400a4c8aea" :question/math-academy-id "q-9944" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)={x}^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡x$ at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={sin}^{3}⁡x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2x)({sin}^{3}⁡x)+({x}^{2})(3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =2x{sin}^{3}⁡x+3{x}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=0} & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot {sin}^{3}⁡(0)+3\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0724f9db-d6c0-545d-9a29-1ca2412734d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x+5)+2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489454} {:answer/id #uuid "53539cff-4e38-5757-b682-d79c166cddc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x+5)+6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489456} {:answer/id #uuid "61d46382-8382-5f5b-b95a-a8283c789b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x+5)+6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489457} {:answer/id #uuid "9813cdaa-2703-5666-9fc7-b7b9102748d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x+5)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489458} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f00326-ca57-54aa-aae9-be28ca8eb4ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x+5)+6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53539cff-4e38-5757-b682-d79c166cddc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x+5)+6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489456} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c704d0-15db-517b-aa14-8aa5c95b21ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489459}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13151117-2915-58a7-8af2-4dc4a9478503" :question/problem "If $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x+5),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x+5).$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x+5).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x+5))+({x}^{2})(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x+5)) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x+5)+6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}]}
